35:Here, we address this gap by combining high-density EEG (HDEEG) with NM to elucidate the electrophysiological heterogeneity of neurodevelopmental and psychiatric disorders. We perform our investigation at two levels, the scalp (the level of electrode) and the sources (the level of cortical brain sources), thanks to the availability of high channel density. We develop an end-to-end NM framework of a normative HD-EEG cohort comprising 560 healthy subjects (age 5 -18 yo), see Fig. 1a for illustrative explanation. We then systematically quantify the heterogeneity observed in the HD-EEG power spectrum and functional connectivity among a clinical group of 1674 age-matched patients diagnosed with ASD, ADHD, anxiety (ANX), and learning disorders (LD), Fig. 1b, c. Inspired by MRI-based studies, we hypothesize that those disorders exhibit substantial electrophysiological heterogeneity and that individual patient deviations are likely to demonstrate low spatial homogeneity across both channels and cortical regions.
45:EEG power spectra (i.e., EEG power in the prede /uniFB01 ned frequency band: delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma) have been the most used features in clinical EEG research [14]. More recently and with the availability of HD-EEG, source-space functional connectivity analysis has emerged as a highly promising approach, establishing a framework for the electrophysiological circuit-level differentiation of healthy and diseased brains [35]. Here, we have developed models for the normative trajectories of these two wellestablished sets of features and assessed the heterogeneity in neurodevelopmental and psychiatric disorders based on subject deviations from the normative trajectories. For each feature, we split our reference population (HC) into training and test sets, and trained a Generalized Additive Model for Location, Scale, and Shape (GAMLSS) on the training set. For the spectral features, a GAMLSS was /uniFB01 tted to the relative power (Welch ' s method) of each channel and each frequency band. Functional connectivity was estimated between 68 prede /uniFB01 ned brain regions from the DesikanKilliany atlas using Amplitude Envelope Correlation -AEC- [36, 37], corrected for source leakage using an orthogonalization approach [38]. A GAMLSS was /uniFB01 tted to the connectivity values at each connection and each frequency band.
47:Subsequently, subjects with ASD, ADHD, ANX, and LD, along with an HC(test) group were projected on these models to calculate their deviation scores ( z -scores, z ). These deviations can be negative (lower than normative values) or positive (higher than normative values), Fig. 2a1, b1. Extreme deviations were de /uniFB01 ned as | z | &gt; 2 [22, 24, 26 -31], Fig. 2a2, b2.
49:First, we computed the percentage of subjects with at least one extremely deviated channel/connection, as well as the number of extremely deviated channels/connections per subject in each group. Next, we evaluated the spatial overlap of extreme deviations (among the subjects with at least one extreme deviation) by computing the percentage of extremely deviated subjects per channel/connection within each group, (Fig. 2a3, b3). A group-based permutation test [22] was used to compare clinical groups and HCs overlap maps (all results were corrected for multiple comparisons using FDR), Fig. 2a4, b4.
51:The distribution of the number of extremely deviated channels/ connections across groups is depicted in Fig. S19 -S28 in Supplementary Materials. Range and median values are reported in Tables S7 -S11 and S13 -S17. The number of negatively deviated channels per subject in all clinical groups were signi /uniFB01 cantly different as compared to HC(test) in delta band ( p &lt; 0.03, MannWhitney test). For the positive deviations, all groups showed signi /uniFB01 cant differences compared to HC(test) at alpha and beta bands ( p &lt; 0.01 ). Regarding functional connectivity features, ADHD and ASD showed signi /uniFB01 cantly fewer numbers of negative extreme deviations compared to HCs, particularly in the beta and gamma bands ( p &lt; 0.01 ). Conversely, ANX and LD demonstrate signi /uniFB01 cant differences only in the alpha band ( p &lt; 0.05 ). However,
