Spectral features . As previously stated, a normative model estimates the relationship between a response variable and one or more covariates. In the context of this study, we are interested in the spectral features of the EEG signal as the designated response variable. This choice was motivated by the large literature about the alterations of EEG power in psychiatric disorders [13]. The power spectrum density (PSD) for each epoch and each channel is computed using Welch ' s method (1-second Hann window with a 50% overlap, and a spectral resolution of 0.5 Hz). PSDs are averaged across all epochs within a single subject. To assess the relative power in speci /uniFB01 c frequency bands (delta [1 -4 Hz], theta [4 -8 Hz], alpha [8 -13 Hz], beta [13 -30 Hz], gamma [30 -45 Hz]), the absolute power within each narrow band is divided by the power within the broader band [1 -45 Hz].

Functional connectivity . EEG-based functional networks were computed using the HD-EEG source connectivity method, as described in [35]. Brie /uniFB02 y, cortical sources are computed using the exact low-resolution brain electromagnetic tomography (eLORETA) which aims to reconstruct the cortical activity from EEG data with correct localization [51]. In our case, the noise covariance matrix was set to the identity matrix, and the regularization parameter λ was set to 0.1 (inversely proportional to the signal-to-noise ratio). Age-speci /uniFB01 c head models of the three layers (brain, skull, and scalp) were built using an MRI template of young children (4 -18 y) [52]. We used the Boundary Element Method (BEM) provided by the MNE Python package. The forward and inverse models were solved within a source space of 4098 sources per hemisphere (with a ~ 5 mm spacing between sources). Then, to streamline the complexity of the cortical sources, we downsampled them to 68 representative sources by averaging the sources within each region as de /uniFB01 ned by the DesikanKilliany atlas [53]. Subsequently, we computed functional connectivity between pairwise regions of interest, using the amplitude envelope correlation (AEC) method, de /uniFB01 ned as the Pearson correlation between signals ' envelopes derived from the Hilbert transform [36, 37]. Zero-lag signal overlaps caused by spatial leakage were removed using a pairwise orthogonalization approach before connectivity computation [38].

Following preprocessing and feature extraction, the cohort was restricted to participants aged 5 to 18 years. Larger diagnostic groups, including ADHD and ANX were downsampled while ensuring a balanced representation across age, sex, and site/study covariates. The /uniFB01 nal dataset used for analysis is described in detail in the data description section.

## Normative modeling

Normative Modeling (NM) seeks to establish a standard or normative relationship between a response variable (behavioral, demographic, or clinical variables) and at least one covariate (a quantitative biological measure, e.g. age, sex). In this context, Generalized Additive Models for Location, Scale, and Shape (GAMLSS) [54], are semi-parametric regression models. In these models, the response variable is presumed to follow a speci /uniFB01 c distribution, wherein the parameters of this distribution can be linked to a set of explanatory variables via linear or nonlinear predictor functions, providing a /uniFB02 exible framework to capture complex relationships. The mathematical formulation of GAMLSS is as follows:

